WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.005
What it doesn’t do well: new knowledge.

2
00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:08.216
The intuition “fine-tune on our documents and the model will know them” is common and mostly wrong.

3
00:00:08.496 --> 00:00:10.672
Two studies are worth knowing by name.

4
00:00:10.952 --> 00:00:18.312
Gekhman and colleagues (2024), “Does Fine-Tuning LLMs on New Knowledge Encourage Hallucinations?

5
00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:22.197
”, ran a controlled experiment with closed-book question answering.

6
00:00:22.477 --> 00:00:31.864
Before training, they sorted each example by whether the base model already knew the answer, then varied the share of unknown answers in the fine-tuning set.

7
00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:33.723
Two findings matter for you.

8
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:41.171
First, examples with new facts were learned much more slowly than examples consistent with what the model already knew.

9
00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:49.173
Second, once the model did fit those new facts, its tendency to hallucinate on held-out test questions grew roughly linearly.

10
00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:57.880
The authors conclude that models acquire facts mostly in pretraining and that fine-tuning mostly teaches them to use what they already have.

11
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.576
Their own caveat: one model (PaLM 2-S) and one task format.

12
00:01:02.856 --> 00:01:07.485
Ovadia and colleagues (2023), “Fine-Tuning or Retrieval?

13
00:01:07.765 --> 00:01:20.715
”, compared continued training on raw text with retrieval for three 7-billion-parameter open models, on subsets of the MMLU benchmark and on a set of questions about events after the models’ training cutoff.

14
00:01:20.995 --> 00:01:27.032
Retrieval consistently beat fine-tuning, both for knowledge the models had seen and for new knowledge.

15
00:01:27.312 --> 00:01:32.069
On the current-events questions, for example, Mistral 7B scored about 0.

16
00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:34.483
48 with no help, about 0.

17
00:01:34.763 --> 00:01:38.517
50 after fine-tuning on the relevant text, and about 0.

18
00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:40.355
88 with retrieval.

19
00:01:40.635 --> 00:01:49.211
Training on ten paraphrases of each fact helped fine-tuning noticeably, which hints at why one pass over a document teaches so little.

20
00:01:49.491 --> 00:01:54.376
In their setup, adding fine-tuning on top of retrieval didn’t beat retrieval alone.

21
00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:59.520
The practical rule follows: facts go in the context, behavior goes in the weights.

22
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:10.360
Retrieval also gives you what fine-tuning can’t: you can update a fact by editing the index, cite where an answer came from, and verify the answer against the retrieved text.
